            "keyPoints": [
                "ISBNdb markets bulk print-book sourcing to AI labs: 1,000 to 1,000,000 books per order, strict NDA, buyer identity and targets never disclosed.",
                "The broker concedes the optics: 'AI company destroys two million books' is not a headline that generates sympathy.",
                "Anthropic internal 2024 docs described the goal as destructively scanning 'all the books in the world' and said 'we don't want it to be known that we are working on this'.",
                "Books were bought used in bulk — the Washington Post named Better World Books and World of Books as suppliers.",
                "A vendor proposal in the unsealed files offered to convert 500,000 to 2,000,000 books over six months; the final processed count stays redacted.",
                "Legal asymmetry: buying and destructively scanning print was ruled fair use, while keeping pirated digital copies cost Anthropic $1.5B.",
                "Correction to the framing: the underlying investigation was published 27 Jan 2026 by the Washington Post; July 2026 is when the ISBNdb broker angle broke.",
                "Anthropic is a named, court-documented buyer — but no current ISBNdb order has been publicly tied to a named AI lab.",
                "Booksellers in the Netherlands and elsewhere report unexplained bulk orders for niche and out-of-print titles."
